	abstract = {Ensuring data confidentiality and integrity in critical infrastructures has become increasingly challenging due to the rise of quantum computing and the limitations of classical cryptographic systems. This paper presents Q-ChainX, a hybrid system, which combines the concepts of Quantum Key Distribution (QKD) with a Post-Quantum Cryptographic (PQC) enabled blockchain, to build ultra secure, tamper resistant and resilient in the future data infrastructures. The suggested architecture consists of two communication layers, namely quantum and classical communication to transmit data and secure keys, respectively, and uses entanglement-based E91 QKD to generate symmetric keys. The cryptographic core supports NTRU and CRYSTALS-Dilithium algorithms to substitute the standard RSA and ECC, which are resistant to quantum attacks. The consensus is obtained by the modified Proof-of-Authority mechanism supported by quantum randomness in the form of QRNGs, to allow a secure and fair election of validators. The key generation rates of a simulated Hyperledger environment simulated in Hyperledger-SimulaQron were found to be over 3800 bps, transaction finality time was found to be 408 ms on 15 nodes, and the consensus success rate was found to be 99.5. The hybrid quantum-PQC mode recorded 99.95 % data integrity and zero key compromise, ranked above the classical set up in all significant security parameters. These findings confirm the practicability of applying Q-ChainX in practice to the smart grids, defense communications, and medical data platforms. The further upgrading will be oriented on the optimization of quantum hardware, expansion of satellite-based QKD, and interoperability with multi-chain ecosystems. Q-ChainX is also a quantum leap to quantum resilient distributed systems, and it guarantees privacy, trust, and sustainability in the era of quantum computing. Β© 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Blockchain Security; Consensus Mechanism; Data Integrity; Post-Quantum Cryptography; Quantum Blockchain; Quantum Key Distribution; Quantum Randomness},
	keywords = {Chains; Computer privacy; Critical infrastructures; Data communication systems; Distributed computer systems; Hybrid systems; Network security; Public key cryptography; Public works; Quantum communication; Quantum computers; Quantum cryptography; Quantum entanglement; Random processes; Block-chain; Blockchain security; Consensus mechanism; Data integrity; Key distribution; Post quantum cryptography; Quantum blockchain; Quantum key; Quantum randomness; Blockchain},
	correspondence_address = {S. Maheboob; Marri Laxman Reddy Institute of Technology and Management, CSE DEPT, Hyderabad, India; email: shaikmaheboob@mlritm.ac.in},
