	pages = {155 - 160},
	doi = {10.1109/SGEE68429.2025.11385622},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034711344?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Corporation Science and Technology General Research Institute Co., Ltd, East China Electric Power Test & Research Institute, Hefei, China},
	abstract = {The rapid expansion of Electric Power IoT (EP-IoT) infrastructures has increased the demand for lightweight yet robust authentication mechanisms capable of withstanding both classical and emerging quantum threats. Traditional deployments rely heavily on SM2-based public-key authentication, which provides regulatory compatibility but lacks long-term security against quantum-capable adversaries. To address this challenge, this paper proposes a collaborative authentication framework that integrates SM2 identity assurance, a lattice-based LAC key encapsulation mechanism, and blockchain-anchored trust verification. The method allows each device to maintain dual cryptographic credentials and binds authentication messages to the latest blockchain state, preventing the reuse of outdated or revoked information. During authentication, SM2 signatures ensure entity legitimacy, while LAC contributes a quantum-resistant shared secret. A unified session key is then derived by combining classical and post-quantum components, providing hybrid security even if one primitive becomes weakened. The resulting protocol offers mutual authentication, strong resistance to replay and impersonation attacks, and compatibility with constrained EP-IoT devices. This work demonstrates a practical pathway for transitioning power systems toward post-quantum security while preserving existing national-cryptography infrastructures. Β© 2025 IEEE.},
