	abstract = {IoT and embedded system firmware updates are becoming more susceptible to online assaults, hacking, and a new quantum threat. This study proposes ChainFort, which is a hybrid blockchain managed framework that incorporates post-quantum cryptography (PQC), federated trust consensus (FTC), and AI-assisted anomaly detection, to safeguard the validation and distribution of firmware. Metadata of the firmware is prehashed and metadata of the network traffic is stored on a permissioned blockchain to permit tamper-proof provenance verification. The PQC layer makes use of CRYSTALS-Dilithium to sign messages and Kyber512 to encrypt them, which is quantum-resistant. ftc is dynamically trusted to nodes by using Proof-of-Federated-Authenticity (PoFA) and swarm-efficient validator selection and balances between decentralization and efficiency. A Blockchain-LSTM Fusion Monitor (BLFM) is an LSTM-based anomaly detection method, which detects the presence of an anomalous firmware behavior to roll back to a verified snapshot. Experimental analysis with sets of IoT firmware and simulated network conditions illustrates 96.8 percent anomaly detection precision, 42 percent less update latency and well-balanced trust-weighted validation of the heterogeneous nodes. The framework proposed provides a holistic, scaleable, and forward-compatible secure firmware management solution, which fills the gap between the conventional blockchain solutions and quantum-resilient, decentralized, IoT-security. Β© 2025 IEEE.},
